WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.440
Remote Control, now you can play the only game show that started in a basement right on your Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:31.960
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.980
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.240
It's just the two of us today, which is unfortunate because it is one of those weird three-player game shows.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.460
There are a bunch of them.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:51.720
I'm not sure who decided three is better than two or four, but I guess maybe it makes the final round thing more interesting when there's not just one versus the other.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.600
Now that I think about it, though, it's not weird that it's a three-player game show.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.360
Most game shows are three players.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:05.520
So instead, I'm going to divert that silly statement and just talk about the back of the box for a second because I do think there was a missed opportunity there.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:13.340
It said, now you can play the only game show that started in a basement right on and it should have been right in your basement, right?

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.280
Like, I feel like that would have been better than just like right on your...

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.140
I feel like I just read a statement that anybody could have put together.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.120
The basement thing would have been a little more clever copywriting.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.980
So that's my editorial note for this episode.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:34.500
Yeah, I'd say that, you know, it would have been more like, you know, that whole like play video games in your basement thing.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:49.440
I think that really didn't come on until the late, like the mid-aughts, or maybe the early aughts, when the whole like neck beard basement dweller meme started to take shape.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.580
Like, the NES was in the living room.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.760
Or you know what? It was in the family room.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.520
Yes, the family room.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:04.000
That is a great point, Sean, because remember, it is just the family computer over in Japan, but it's also the entertainment system here.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.620
It's supposed to be all-encompassing, even if it just has really a gun, a dance pad, and a robot.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.740
Those are all fun things for the family.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.280
Every American family wants a gun, a robot, and a dance pad.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.720
I sure do, still.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:27.360
Now, were you aware that remote control was going to be anything related to a game show that happened to also be a real game show on MTV?

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:40.580
Or were you expecting, I don't know, I was expecting a RC Pro-Am-like game where you drive remote control cars, or maybe you fiddle with an NES controller and it's your remote control.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.680
Anyway, what were you thinking?

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:49.000
I had no idea that this was a game show, let alone an MTV game show.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:03:03.980
So, I sort of figured that it was going to be something like a TV-themed side-scroller, and there'd be weird...

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.020
I don't know what I'm trying to describe here.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:13.160
There'd be lots of the colored bars in the background, and that'd be the things that you jump on.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:19.420
I don't know if you're going to get stuck in a TV, or if it was based off of a really obscure 80s movie.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.220
But I figured there was going to be something along those lines, and not a game show.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:32.640
Yeah, and what's weird is that this isn't a game show that you'll find again, or hear about really again.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:40.140
In fact, while the video game version is coming out, the game show on MTV is being cancelled.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.200
So, it's like a really bad time to release the game, I guess, unless they're saying, like,

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:49.040
we're cancelling the game show on TV because now anybody can play it anywhere.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:55.560
We've reached peak remote control because the premise of the show is that Ken Ober, the host,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.160
wants to be a game show host like his idols, who he has pictures of all over the basement.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:09.680
And so, he set up a game show in his basement, has three people in these kind of, like, lazy boy-like chairs.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.560
I can't tell if they're supposed to be, like, strapped against their will or not, or if they're just happy to be there.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.940
That part wasn't clear to me.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:25.500
But then, like, you know, you're just playing trivia from a TV where you tune into what they're calling TV channels,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:26.640
but are really categories.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.080
And categories are based on either TV shows or pop culture-related things.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.760
And that's always a recipe for success, something that will never age poorly.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.780
Everybody will always understand these questions for infinity, right?

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:46.720
You definitely got every single thing they were referencing when you got to the trivia portions.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.800
Yeah, I'll be honest.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.240
I didn't really get many of these.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.460
Probably the majority, no.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:00.080
But, like, did you watch this show at all?

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:05.060
I'd never heard of the show before this, but yes, I did wind up pulling up.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.500
I think there's, like, a full episode available on YouTube.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.160
Oh, there's a lot.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:10.560
Okay, great. Yeah, sorry.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:15.460
I just, like, Googled it, and I pulled up the episode from June 28th, 1989.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:24.060
So what I watched was just about as strange, stylistically, as the NES game that we're playing.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:32.640
So what I thought was a series of, like, you know, weird art direction decisions actually are totally on brand for the show itself.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.420
Yeah, so I was kind of pleasantly surprised with the show, like, the real show.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:44.440
Like, it had a young Colin Quinn on it, which I thought was strange.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:55.680
I just remember him being, like, a really upset, like, asshole on Comedy Central with, like, a tough crowd.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.360
And then, like, what's his, uh, Adam Sandler makes appearances.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:04.860
I've never recognized the actual, like, uh, host.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:11.340
But, yeah, it does, it does, like, bear some resemblances in how it's presented.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:16.720
But I, it, it's definitely just a very abridged version of that.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:29.700
Like, most of what goes on in this show is sort of trying to be encapsulated into, um, when you select a category in the game, it'll have some, like, off-color remark about stuff.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:39.440
And then the same kind of humor in the, uh, in the actual channel names or, uh, categories.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:47.980
Yeah, there's, like, a sketch comedy component to the whole thing where there's not, like, a overarching plot through the episode, per se.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:55.940
No. But, uh, there is a, um, you know, it's more than just, uh, Jeopardy, pick the category, here's the question.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:08.760
There's a lot of, uh, and I can't tell you if it's improv or scripted or not, but there's a lot of work that goes into, uh, the setup here, which makes this kind of, uh, I guess, you know, what MTV was going for was, like,

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.980
this is the new kind of game show, right?

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:13.100
Yeah. This is, like, it's game show plus comedy.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:17.620
Very irreverent and, like, breaking the rules of game shows.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.440
Like, maybe this was the first one.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.640
I don't know. Maybe there, maybe it wasn't.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.980
But it seems like, oh, this, this is, like, it's not your parents' game show.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.220
You know, very MTV, like, uh, of them to do it.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:36.340
Yes. And, you know, there's a, there's a very weird aesthetic to the whole thing as well.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:47.480
Both the show and the video game, um, there's a lot of just, you know, it's because it's in, it's in a basement, but there's a giant Pez dispenser of the guy who was the host of the newlywed show.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:59.400
There's, um, uh, that you, when you, uh, when you lose, your chair goes flinging through, uh, what I imagine is a very easily breakable wall, uh, to be ejected from.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:05.400
From, and, uh, there's the, the game show host's mother kind of, like, chimes in from time to time.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.860
Oh, didn't know that. Yeah, not, not in our-

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.880
That must have been season two. Yeah, it wasn't in our video game, uh, either, uh, to the best of my knowledge.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:13.860
That's the thing.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.380
So, we're missing, I think that's what we're missing in the video game.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:33.300
Yeah. Format here is we're missing a lot, like, they're still trying to, I don't know, it's like aggressive, self-deprecating humor in, in the video game still, but there isn't the, uh, the sort of sketch comedy or, um, you know, intricacy of the actual show.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.540
I don't know, it doesn't even really look like it's in a basement, right?

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:43.600
Like, I get that those are couches that they're sitting on, but it doesn't really feel like we, you know, put us in a basement that's recognizable.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.200
Give me a washer dryer or something.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:52.980
Uh, yeah, the, so when they're, when they're showing the contestants like that looks, it looks close.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:09:03.980
But when you're showing the host, like the TV's right, the TV, cause it's like so, uh, off the wall design and like very, uh, exaggerated.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:13.700
But everything else is just like, there wasn't just a scrolling like MTV, uh, banner in the back that was just a bunch of MTV logos.

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:19.700
It was still like the same kind of set design and it, it kind of looked like a missed opportunity there.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:25.460
And MTV and trademarks and, um, incorporated things are like all over this.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:32.680
There's so much like legal stuff on, on this game where like MTV is both on the trivia thing as a like repeating banner.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:38.460
It's, uh, it's also like the remote control trademark is everywhere.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.380
I don't know if you need like the trademark in game.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:44.980
Like I get that you need that on the legal screen, but it just feels weird to have it all over the place.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:54.160
And while we're talking about, uh, kind of, I guess like the legalities and everything, this game does have a very, uh, funny accompanying manual.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.440
Oh. That, that gets into a lot of that too.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.820
And so I, I'm sure you, this is probably one that you didn't read.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.000
